WEBVTT
NOTE why-does-rice-get-sticky - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.270
A grain sold as glutinous contains no
gluten whatsoever.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:08.176
Rice gets sticky because cooking makes its
starch granules swell and break down, and

00:00:08.176 --> 00:00:12.848
starch released at the grain surface acts
as a glue between neighbouring grains.

00:00:12.848 --> 00:00:17.221
The word glutinous describes a texture,
glue-like, not an ingredient.

00:00:17.221 --> 00:00:22.101
Gluten is a wheat protein, and rice
stickiness is not a protein story at all.

00:00:22.101 --> 00:00:27.116
It is a starch story built from two
molecules, which chemical databases such

00:00:27.116 --> 00:00:31.141
as PubChem list as separate entries:
amylose and amylopectin.

00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:32.441
That much is settled.

00:00:32.441 --> 00:00:37.270
What it does not settle is why the same
two molecules give you loose, separate

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:42.347
grains of basmati in one pot and a single
cohesive mass of sticky rice in another.

00:00:42.347 --> 00:00:43.749
Why does rice get sticky

00:00:43.749 --> 00:00:47.197
Start with what is inside a grain of
milled white rice.

00:00:47.197 --> 00:00:52.400
Most of it is endosperm, the stored food
the seed would have used to grow, and most

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.155
of that endosperm is starch.

00:00:54.155 --> 00:00:57.286
Proteins are present, but they are minor
partners here.

00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:01.727
Wheat dough becomes elastic because two
families of wheat proteins link into a

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.744
stretchy network when the flour is wetted
and worked.

00:01:04.744 --> 00:01:06.516
Rice has no comparable network.

00:01:06.516 --> 00:01:11.204
So when rice clumps, the glue has to be
coming from somewhere else, and every line

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:13.091
of evidence points to the starch.

00:01:13.091 --> 00:01:15.172
Starch is not one substance.

00:01:15.172 --> 00:01:21.341
It is glucose, linked into long chains,
and those chains come in two

00:01:21.341 --> 00:01:25.869
Amylose is mostly linear: a long chain
that can coil into a helix.

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:30.769
Amylopectin is heavily branched, a bushy
molecule with many short chains hanging

00:01:30.769 --> 00:01:33.832
off a backbone, and it is far larger than
amylose.

00:01:33.832 --> 00:01:38.190
Both are catalogued by PubChem, the
chemical database run under the National

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.395
Institutes of Health.

00:01:39.395 --> 00:01:44.549
Inside the grain, these molecules are
packed into tiny starch granules, and rice

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:48.093
granules are among the smallest of the
cereal starches.

00:01:48.093 --> 00:01:52.878
Within each granule, the branches of
amylopectin line up side by side into

00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:58.309
ordered, crystalline layers, with amylose
threaded through the less ordered regions.

00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:02.330
That ordered packing is the key to
everything that follows.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.827
A raw granule is dense, partly crystalline
and resistant to water.

00:02:06.827 --> 00:02:11.954
Uncooked rice is hard and not sticky,
because its starch is locked away in that

00:02:11.954 --> 00:02:12.603
structure.

00:02:12.603 --> 00:02:16.698
Stickiness only appears when that
structure is taken apart, and the methods

00:02:16.698 --> 00:02:19.701
that show it happening are worth looking
at one by one.

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:24.677
The process is called gelatinization, and
it was not inferred from texture alone.

00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:27.729
Three instruments show it from different
angles.

00:02:27.729 --> 00:02:32.688
Under polarized light, a raw starch
granule glows with a dark cross across it,

00:02:32.688 --> 00:02:36.629
called a Maltese cross, because its
ordered layers bend light.

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:41.093
As the granule is heated in water, the
cross fades and disappears.

00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:46.179
X-ray diffraction gives the same verdict
in another language: raw cereal starch

00:02:46.179 --> 00:02:51.266
scatters X-rays into a characteristic
pattern of sharp rings, and after heating

00:02:51.266 --> 00:02:54.613
in water that pattern collapses into a
diffuse blur.

00:02:54.613 --> 00:02:59.121
Differential scanning calorimetry measures
heat flow, and it records a distinct

00:02:59.121 --> 00:03:02.144
absorption of energy at the moment the
crystals melt.

00:03:02.144 --> 00:03:06.309
Three independent signals, one conclusion:
the crystalline order is lost.

00:03:06.309 --> 00:03:09.293
Here is a detail that surprises many
cooks.

00:03:09.293 --> 00:03:12.971
Rice starch gelatinizes well below the
boiling point.

00:03:12.971 --> 00:03:17.732
Water at sea level boils at 100 degrees
Celsius, and a pot of simmering rice sits

00:03:17.732 --> 00:03:21.965
close to that, but the granules begin
swelling and losing their order at

00:03:21.965 --> 00:03:23.728
noticeably lower temperatures.

00:03:23.728 --> 00:03:28.710
Different varieties gelatinize at
different temperatures, and rice breeders

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.629
classify grains as low, intermediate or
high on that scale.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:37.627
The published values vary with variety and
method, so this script will not quote a

00:03:37.627 --> 00:03:38.480
single figure.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.265
What matters is that by the time the pot
is simmering, gelatinization is well under

00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:44.544
way.

00:03:44.544 --> 00:03:48.408
It feels like the subject gets larger
instead of smaller.

00:03:48.408 --> 00:03:51.323
One answer rearranges everything around
it.

00:03:51.323 --> 00:03:56.332
Once the crystals melt, water floods in
and the granules swell, many times their

00:03:56.332 --> 00:03:57.333
original volume.

00:03:57.333 --> 00:03:58.705
Swelling is not tidy.

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:04.189
As the granules swell, some starch leaches
out, both from granules at the surface of

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:08.368
each grain and from the fine starch dust
left behind by milling.

00:04:08.368 --> 00:04:13.675
That released starch forms a thin, viscous
film on the outside of every grain.

00:04:13.675 --> 00:04:18.352
When cooking stops and steam escapes, the
surface film dries and thickens.

00:04:18.352 --> 00:04:23.211
Where two grains touch, their films merge,
and the grains bond where films touch.

00:04:23.211 --> 00:04:28.502
Electron microscopes show this directly:
images of cooked rice surfaces reveal a

00:04:28.502 --> 00:04:32.669
layer of smeared, gelled material that is
absent on raw grains.

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:37.687
Food chemists publishing in journals such
as the Journal of Agricultural and Food

00:04:37.687 --> 00:04:42.209
Chemistry, from the American Chemical
Society, have analysed that surface

00:04:42.209 --> 00:04:46.669
material and linked its quantity and
composition to measured stickiness.

00:04:46.669 --> 00:04:50.643
This is also why rinsing rice before
cooking can change the result.

00:04:50.643 --> 00:04:55.377
Rinsing washes away loose starch dust from
the outside of milled grains, so less

00:04:55.377 --> 00:04:58.573
material is available to form that first
surface film.

00:04:58.573 --> 00:05:03.493
It does not alter the starch locked inside
each grain, which still gelatinizes and

00:05:03.493 --> 00:05:06.013
still releases some starch during cooking.

00:05:06.013 --> 00:05:10.601
Studies of rinsing report effects that
differ by variety and by how thoroughly

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.659
the rice is washed, so the honest summary
is modest.

00:05:13.659 --> 00:05:18.037
Rinsing reduces one source of glue. It
does not remove the mechanism.

00:05:18.037 --> 00:05:23.525
So why is glutinous rice so much stickier
than basmati, if both gelatinize and both

00:05:23.525 --> 00:05:24.517
release starch?

00:05:24.517 --> 00:05:29.245
The answer lies in the ratio of the two
molecules, and much of that ratio is

00:05:29.245 --> 00:05:30.987
controlled by a single gene.

00:05:30.987 --> 00:05:36.405
It is called the Waxy gene, and it encodes
an enzyme called granule-bound starch

00:05:36.405 --> 00:05:39.792
synthase, which builds amylose inside the
granule.

00:05:39.792 --> 00:05:44.363
When that enzyme is broken, without it,
the grain makes almost no amylose.

00:05:44.363 --> 00:05:47.237
Its starch is almost entirely amylopectin.

00:05:47.237 --> 00:05:51.344
Glutinous rice varieties carry broken
versions of this gene.

00:05:51.344 --> 00:05:56.540
Rice geneticists, including those at the
USDA Agricultural Research Service, which

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:01.545
breeds rice and studies its grain quality,
use the Waxy gene as one of the main

00:06:01.545 --> 00:06:04.587
markers for predicting how a new line will
cook.

00:06:04.587 --> 00:06:07.397
The oldest way to see this needs no
sequencer.

00:06:07.397 --> 00:06:11.856
Iodine slips inside the helical coil of
amylose and turns it a deep blue.

00:06:11.856 --> 00:06:16.513
Amylopectin, with its short branches,
cannot hold iodine the same way, and

00:06:16.513 --> 00:06:18.464
stains a reddish-brown instead.

00:06:18.464 --> 00:06:23.483
Cut a grain of ordinary rice, add iodine,
and it turns dark blue.

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:27.027
Do the same with glutinous rice and it
turns reddish-brown.

00:06:27.027 --> 00:06:31.173
Measured carefully, with a
spectrophotometer reading the depth of the

00:06:31.173 --> 00:06:35.619
colour, this reaction becomes a standard
laboratory method in which colour

00:06:35.619 --> 00:06:39.944
estimates amylose content, used by
breeding programmes and grain-quality

00:06:39.944 --> 00:06:40.725
laboratories.

00:06:40.725 --> 00:06:43.806
Across those sources a consistent pattern
appears.

00:06:43.806 --> 00:06:48.859
Varieties with relatively high amylose,
such as many long-grain types, cook firmer

00:06:48.859 --> 00:06:49.968
and more separate.

00:06:49.968 --> 00:06:55.388
Varieties with lower amylose, such as many
short-grain types, cook softer and

00:06:55.388 --> 00:06:56.021
stickier.

00:06:56.021 --> 00:06:59.535
Glutinous rice, with almost no amylose,
sits at the extreme.

00:06:59.535 --> 00:07:03.752
Researchers publishing on starch
structure, and bodies such as the Royal

00:07:03.752 --> 00:07:08.086
Society of Chemistry that cover the
chemistry of starch, describe the same

00:07:08.086 --> 00:07:11.835
direction of effect, even where their
exact measurements differ.

00:07:11.835 --> 00:07:14.482
Why would amylose make grains less sticky?

00:07:14.482 --> 00:07:19.273
Part of the answer comes from viscosity
instruments, such as the Rapid Visco

00:07:19.273 --> 00:07:24.190
Analyser, which heat a starch slurry while
stirring and record viscosity as it

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:24.757
thickens.

00:07:24.757 --> 00:07:29.158
The procedure is to heat a starch slurry
while stirring, record viscosity as it

00:07:29.158 --> 00:07:31.387
thickens, then cool it and record again.

00:07:31.387 --> 00:07:37.792
On cooling, the paste firms up, a rise
called setback, and setback tracks

00:07:37.792 --> 00:07:43.108
Linear amylose chains, freed from the
granule, quickly find each other and pack

00:07:43.108 --> 00:07:47.077
back into ordered bundles, a process
called retrogradation.

00:07:47.077 --> 00:07:52.286
That rapid re-ordering firms the grain and
its surface, so the film between grains

00:07:52.286 --> 00:07:56.160
sets into something closer to a firm skin
than a tacky paste.

00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:58.326
Amylopectin behaves differently.

00:07:58.326 --> 00:08:04.013
Its bushy branches swell more freely, hold
water and re-order only slowly, over days

00:08:04.013 --> 00:08:05.232
rather than hours.

00:08:05.232 --> 00:08:10.263
A grain rich in amylopectin stays soft,
swollen and tacky at the surface, which is

00:08:10.263 --> 00:08:13.269
exactly the texture glutinous rice is
prized for.

00:08:13.269 --> 00:08:17.861
The same retrogradation explains a
familiar kitchen observation: day-old rice

00:08:17.861 --> 00:08:22.811
from the refrigerator is harder and less
sticky than fresh rice, because its starch

00:08:22.811 --> 00:08:24.123
has partly re-ordered.

00:08:24.123 --> 00:08:28.000
Variety sets the range, but the method
moves the result within it.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:32.411
Long-grain rice tends to cook firmer and
more separate, while glutinous rice cooks

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:36.339
soft and tacky, and no amount of technique
fully swaps one for the other.

00:08:36.339 --> 00:08:40.455
More water lets granules swell further and
release more starch.

00:08:40.455 --> 00:08:45.748
Stirring rubs grains together and scrapes
starch off their surfaces, which is why

00:08:45.748 --> 00:08:49.080
risotto, made with constant stirring,
turns creamy.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:53.258
Resting after cooking lets steam
redistribute and the surface film set.

00:08:53.258 --> 00:08:57.789
Laboratories quantify the outcome with
texture analysers, which press a probe

00:08:57.789 --> 00:09:02.496
into cooked rice and measure the force
needed to pull it away, alongside trained

00:09:02.496 --> 00:09:03.379
tasting panels.

00:09:03.379 --> 00:09:07.586
When the cross-section glows, watch the
granules, not the grain.

00:09:07.586 --> 00:09:12.451
The grain barely changes shape, while its
starch granules swell and spill.

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:17.294
Every instrument in this episode,
polarized light, X-ray diffraction,

00:09:17.294 --> 00:09:22.699
calorimetry, iodine and viscosity, is
looking at that same small event from a

00:09:22.699 --> 00:09:23.752
different side.

00:09:23.752 --> 00:09:28.379
This evidence establishes that stickiness
comes from starch, and that amylose

00:09:28.379 --> 00:09:30.061
content predicts much of it.

00:09:30.061 --> 00:09:34.747
It does not give a single number that
defines stickiness: instruments and

00:09:34.747 --> 00:09:39.240
tasting panels measure different things,
and they do not always agree.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:43.573
Two varieties with similar amylose can
still cook differently, because

00:09:43.573 --> 00:09:48.773
amylopectin chain lengths, protein and fat
content also play roles that remain under

00:09:48.773 --> 00:09:49.144
study.

00:09:49.144 --> 00:09:51.919
How much rinsing changes the result
remains open.

00:09:51.919 --> 00:09:55.656
And none of this evidence says anything
about nutrition or health.

00:09:55.656 --> 00:09:59.512
A word that sounds like wheat turns out to
describe a missing enzyme.

00:09:59.512 --> 00:10:04.121
The glue on your rice is starch that
escaped its granule, and how much escapes

00:10:04.121 --> 00:10:06.899
was written, in large part, into a single
gene.
